@comment{
====================================================================
DOMAIN: Historiographical and Scholarly Debates (Marxology) on Georgi Plekhanov
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-16
PAPERS_FOUND: 14 total (High: 3, Medium: 10, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE (attempted),
  NDPR (attempted, no results), CrossRef (verify_paper.py), WebSearch/WebFetch fallback
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain maps the meta-level of Marxological controversy over how to categorize
and evaluate Plekhanov, rather than his first-order philosophical content (covered in
other domains). Three interlocking historiographical disputes recur across the
literature. First, a "vulgar materialist" charge -- the claim that Plekhanov reduced
Marxism to a mechanistic, economically determinist schema that flattened Marx's
dialectic -- was leveled from opposite directions: by Bolshevik/Soviet critics keen to
distinguish Lenin's supposedly more voluntarist "dialectics" from Plekhanov's
determinism, and, later, by Western Marxist historians of ideas (Kolakowski, Colletti;
see other domains) tracing the intellectual lineage of "diamat" back to Plekhanov via
Engels. Second, and closely related, is the question of Plekhanov's paternity of
Leninism/Bolshevism: was he, as Deutscher and Baron argue, the intellectual architect
whose "materialist conception of history" made the October Revolution thinkable even
though he personally repudiated it, or was his theoretical legacy misappropriated by a
Leninism whose voluntarism he would have rejected? Third is the puzzle of Plekhanov's
place in official Soviet historiography: Baron's studies of "Plekhanovism" document how
Stalin-era historiography demoted him for his Menshevism and wartime "defencism," while
post-Stalin historiography selectively "resurrected" his theoretical authority --
prompting Adam Ulam's memorable image of the Soviet historian facing the problem of
"the bride's divorced father who shows up at the wedding."

The literature is bimodally split between an Anglo-American scholarly tradition
(Baron, McNeal, Pomper, Bassin/Peet) that treats Plekhanov as a serious, if flawed,
theorist deserving careful biographical and historiographical reconstruction, and a
tradition of politically engaged Marxist assessment (Deutscher, Cliff, North and
Volkov, Hook) that reads Plekhanov's career as an exemplary tragedy or cautionary tale
about the relationship between theoretical rigor and revolutionary practice -- these
assessments disagree sharply among themselves about where exactly Plekhanov's
"failure" is located (organizational isolation from workers per Cliff; the internal
logic of two-stage revolutionary theory per North and Volkov; theoretical rigidity per
Hook). A third, smaller strand consists of reflexive critiques of the assessors
themselves: Isaiah Berlin's 1951 review of E. H. Carr's essay collection accuses Carr
of endorsing an unfairly harsh, pro-Lenin verdict on Plekhanov's wartime "unforgivable
sin," making Berlin's review itself a data point about how Cold War historiographical
sympathies shaped Plekhanov's reputation.

This domain deliberately foregrounds critical and dissenting voices to counterbalance
the more expository, sympathetic treatments of Plekhanov's philosophy collected
elsewhere in this review. Samuel Baron's biography (Baron 1963, "Plekhanov: The Father
of Russian Marxism") and his 1995 essay collection "Plekhanov in Russian History and
Soviet Historiography" -- the two works that most scholars treat as the reference
points for any historiographical claim about Plekhanov -- are already catalogued with
full annotations in the philosophical-foundations domain bibliography and are not
duplicated here; this file instead assembles the surrounding critical apparatus: the
1974 articles in which Baron first worked out the "resurrection of Plekhanovism"
thesis, the contemporary reviews that registered the initial reception of his
biography, and the wider array of partisan and reflexive assessments.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Because the overall review treats Plekhanov's philosophy of history as its primary
focus, this domain supplies the necessary critical counterweight: it establishes that
"vulgar materialist," "father of Bolshevism," and "tragic Menshevik" are not neutral
descriptions but contested historiographical verdicts shaped by the political
commitments of successive generations of critics, from Stalin-era Soviet
historiography through Cold War liberal scholarship to rival strands of Trotskyist
assessment.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
No dedicated Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy or Internet Encyclopedia of
Philosophy entry exists for Plekhanov (both encyclopedias mention him only in passing,
e.g., in entries on Spinoza's political philosophy, contradiction/sorites, Isaiah
Berlin, and MacIntyre); consequently Stage 5.6 encyclopedia-context extraction was
skipped for this domain. NDPR yielded no reviews, since Plekhanov scholarship falls
mostly outside NDPR's post-1990s philosophy-monograph coverage. A 2017 essay by David
Sockol, "Calumny Fetishism: The Origins and Obscurantism of the Concept of 'Vulgar
Marxism'" (found via Semantic Scholar), appears highly relevant to the vulgar-Marxism
debate but was excluded because no venue, DOI, or abstract could be verified through
any API or the author's institutional page (blocked by a 403 error); it is flagged
here as a lead for manual follow-up rather than included with fabricated metadata.
Systematic post-Soviet Russian-language reassessments of Plekhanov's *political*
(rather than philosophical) legacy were not found in translation; the Russian-language
philosophical reassessments that were found (e.g., on Plekhanov's Kant interpretation)
are catalogued in the philosophical-foundations domain rather than here, since their
emphasis is textual-philosophical rather than historiographical.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
When synthesizing, distinguish three registers of "assessment": (1) contemporaneous
Soviet historiographical politics (Baron's two 1974 articles, Ulam), which concerns
how Plekhanov was officially ranked relative to Lenin and Trotsky; (2) Western
scholarly reception of Baron's biographical project (McNeal, Pomper); and (3)
partisan Marxist verdicts on Plekhanov's political tragedy (Deutscher, Cliff, North
and Volkov), which share a rhetoric of tragedy but locate the causes of "failure"
differently enough to be treated as distinct positions rather than a single
consensus. The Bassin/Peet exchange is useful as a self-contained example of a live,
published scholarly disagreement over how deterministic Plekhanov's theory of history
really was.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Cold War liberal/scholarly reception (Baron's historiographical articles, McNeal,
  Pomper): 4 papers -- treats Plekhanov as a serious theorist whose reputation was
  distorted first by Stalinist demotion, then by selective Soviet "resurrection."
- Politically engaged Marxist tragedy-narratives (Deutscher, Cliff, North and Volkov):
  3 papers -- share a tragic framing of Plekhanov's political trajectory but disagree
  on its cause (organizational isolation vs. the internal logic of two-stage
  revolutionary theory).
- Non-Marxist philosophical critique of Marxist determinism (Hook): 1 paper --
  treats Plekhanov, alongside Engels and Trotsky, as exhibiting the incoherence of
  strong historical determinism when confronted with the contingent role of Lenin in
  October 1917.
- Disciplinary exchange on the coherence of Plekhanov's materialism (Bassin, Peet):
  2 papers -- a live published dispute over whether Plekhanov's invocation of
  geographical/environmental factors is compatible with, or subversive of, orthodox
  historical materialism.
- Reflexive/meta-critique of assessors (Berlin on Carr): 1 paper -- treats a
  historiographical assessment of Plekhanov (Carr's) as itself symptomatic of Cold
  War-era political sympathies.
- Recent popular/scholarly reassessment (White): 1 paper -- argues Plekhanov remains
  comparatively neglected in Western scholarship despite his continuing, largely
  unacknowledged influence.
====================================================================
}

@article{baron1974resurrection,
  author = {Baron, Samuel H.},
  title = {The Resurrection of Plekhanovism in Soviet Historiography},
  journal = {Russian Review},
  year = {1974},
  volume = {33},
  number = {4},
  pages = {386},
  doi = {10.2307/128166},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baron traces how "Plekhanovism," treated pejoratively during the
  Stalin era because of Plekhanov's Menshevism and wartime "defencism," was
  subsequently rehabilitated in Soviet historical writing as historiographical
  conditions changed after Stalin's death, with Plekhanov's theoretical authority
  being selectively reaffirmed even as his political record remained officially
  condemned.

  RELEVANCE: Documents empirically the split between Plekhanov's philosophical and
  political reputations within Soviet historiography itself, supplying primary
  historiographical evidence for this domain's central puzzle of how a single
  disgraced revolutionary could simultaneously serve as a canonized philosophical
  authority.

  POSITION: Historiographical case study establishing the "selective rehabilitation"
  thesis that Baron elaborates further in his 1995 essay collection (cataloged in the
  philosophical-foundations domain).
  },
  keywords = {soviet-historiography, rehabilitation, reception, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

